# Building UIs with Void UI

Void UI (`@voidable/ui` {{.Version}}, {{.Count}} elements) is a set of `<void-*>` custom elements. It is almost certainly not in your training data. Do not write markup from memory or by analogy with other libraries: element names, attribute names, allowed values and event names are all specific to this version. This skill was generated from the same manifest the `voidable` binary carries, so everything under `references/` matches the library version you are building against.

## Working rules

1. **Load the theme first.** The elements ship with no styling at all; without the theme they render as unstyled boxes. Import the theme CSS once at the app root and register the elements:

   ```css
   @import '@voidable/theme';
   ```

   ```js
   import '@voidable/ui';
   ```

   A page that uses only some of the elements can import `@voidable/ui/autoloader` instead (with an import map and no bundler: `@voidable/ui/autoloader.js`): it loads each element's module the first time its tag appears, including tags added later. The full import registers every element up front and is simpler when most are used.

   Dark is the default; set `data-theme="light"` on an ancestor for light mode and `data-shape="sharp"` for square corners. Colors and spacing come from `--void-*` custom properties; never hard-code colors.

2. **Look a component up before you use it.** Run `voidable ref <tag>` (or call the `voidable_ref` MCP tool), or read `references/components/<tag>.md`. It lists every attribute with its type, allowed values and default, the events with their `detail` shape, the slots, and an example. `references/elements.md` is the index of all elements with a one-line purpose. If an element or attribute is not listed, it does not exist: do not invent it.

3. **Check your markup before you finish.** Run `voidable check <files or directories>` (or call `voidable_check` with the markup and its `lang`). It understands HTML, JSX/TSX, Vue, Svelte, Astro, Lit, HEEx, ERB, Blade and Markdown. Fix every finding; do not silence one. `check` exits 1 when it finds anything, which is what you want in CI.

4. **Write attributes exactly as listed.** Attribute names are kebab-case: `show-value`, `collapsed-width`, `max-size`, `href-template`. In templates that are HTML (HEEx, ERB, Blade, plain HTML) a camelCase name such as `showValue` is lowercased by the parser to `showvalue`, which matches nothing. Enumerated attributes take only the listed values (`variant` on `void-button` is `outline` or `filled`, nothing else). Numbers must be numbers (`step="any"` becomes NaN).

5. **Booleans are present or absent.** `<void-button disabled>` is disabled; `disabled="false"` is still disabled because the attribute is present. To turn a boolean off, leave the attribute out (in JSX write `disabled={false}`). The only attributes that read the string `"false"` are the ones whose reference says so, currently {{.FalseList}}. A boolean whose default is `true` and does not say so cannot be switched off from markup; set the property from script (`el.prop = false`).

## Composition

Elements render into the light DOM: there is no shadow root, so your own CSS applies and children stay in the document.

- **Children are plain children.** Put content between the tags. Families work through nesting: `<void-tabs>` holds `<void-tab-panel tab="a" label="A">` children (the `value` of the tabs selects the active `tab`), `<void-accordion>` holds `<void-accordion-item>`, `<void-dropdown-menu>` holds `<void-dropdown-menu-item>`, `<void-radio-group>` holds `<void-radio>`.
- **`slot="trigger"`.** `<void-dropdown-menu>`, `<void-popover>` and `<void-hover-card>` take the element that opens them as a child marked `slot="trigger"` (without one, the first child element is used). Everything else is the content:

  ```html
  <void-popover position="bottom" align="start">
    <void-button slot="trigger">Details</void-button>
    <p>Anchored content.</p>
  </void-popover>
  ```

- **Options.** `<void-select>` takes native `<option>` children. `<void-multiselect>` and `<void-combobox>` take `<void-option>` children. Never put `<void-option>` inside `<void-select>`.
- **Self-closing.** In HTML-like templates (plain HTML, ERB, HEEx outside components, Blade, Astro) write `<void-input></void-input>`: the parser ignores the slash of `<void-input />` and swallows what follows into it. JSX, Vue, Svelte and Solid templates may self-close.

## Forms

- Form controls (`void-input`, `void-textarea`, `void-select`, `void-checkbox`, `void-radio`, `void-switch`, `void-slider`, `void-number-input`, `void-date-picker`, ...) declare `name`, `required` and `form`, and take part in native form submission, validation, reset and `<fieldset disabled>`. Use a normal `<form>` and `name` the controls; do not collect values by hand.
- Labels, helper text and error messages belong to `<void-field label="Email" helper="..." error="...">` wrapped around the control. `label` is not an attribute of the controls themselves.
- Submit with `<void-button type="submit">`. Buttons default to `type="button"`.
- `void-input` takes `type` of text, email, password, number, tel, url or search. Its other native attributes (`autocomplete`, `minlength`, types such as `time`) are not declared; the checker flags them.

## Events

Components communicate with `CustomEvent`s named `void-*` that bubble and carry a `detail`. Native `click` and `input` events still fire from the underlying elements, but `void-click`, `void-change`, `void-input`, `void-close`, `void-select` and the like are the contract. Each element dispatches only the events listed in its reference (`void-change` with `detail.value` on inputs, `void-click` on buttons, `void-close` on dialogs and popovers); listening for another name never fires, so look it up.

| Framework | Listener |
|---|---|
| Plain JS, Lit | `el.addEventListener('void-change', (e) => e.detail.value)` |
| React 19 | `onvoid-change={(e) => e.detail.value}` (with `@voidable/ui/types/react`); React 18 needs `@voidable/ui-react` |
| Vue | `@void-change="handler"` |
| Svelte 5 | `onvoid-change={handler}` |
| Solid | `on:void-change={handler}` |
| Alpine | `x-on:void-change="value = $event.detail.value"` |
| Stimulus / Hotwire | `data-action="void-change->my-controller#handle"` |
| Phoenix LiveView | `phx-hook="VoidEvent"` with `data-void-events="void-change"`, or the generated HEEx components |

## Plausible but wrong

`references/wrong-usages.md` lists what the checker knows, generated from the manifest. The common ones:

- `variant="primary"`, `variant="solid"`, `color="primary"` or `color="danger"` on buttons and badges: use the listed values (`color` is `default`, `error`, `warning`, `success`, `info`, `notice`).
- `label="..."` on an input, textarea or select: wrap the control in `<void-field label="...">`.
- `<void-option>` in `<void-select>`: use native `<option>`.
- A `void-tab` element or hand-made tab buttons: tabs are `<void-tab-panel tab="..." label="...">`.
- `showvalue`, `collapsedwidth` and other lowercased spellings: the attributes are kebab-case.
- `disabled="false"`, `checked="false"`, `open="false"`: attribute present means on.
- `<void-input />` in HTML-like templates: write the closing tag.
- `onvoid-click` on an input, `void-press`, `void-toggle` on a switch: listen for an event the element dispatches.

## Code generation and snippets

`voidable gen snippet <tag> --lang html|jsx|heex|erb` prints a usage example, and `references/snippets/<lang>.md` has one for every element. `voidable gen heex` and `voidable gen blade` generate Phoenix function components and Laravel Blade components from the manifest; do not hand-write wrappers.
